Well, partially; about half of my software
development career was spent maintaining other
people's buggy software.

It is an unfortunate facet of the commercial
software "drive to be first to market" that buggy
software gets released buggy by management
direction.

For another example from the world-class punching
dummy for creating buggy software, I once opened a
Microsoft C compiler. Right there in the box, packed
with every one bought, was a list of 125 _known
bugs_ in the compiler my employer had just bought
with good money. No wonder the Microsoft end user
license denies that their products are fit for any
use whatever.

NetHack doesn't suffer from any need to race to
market -- what, is Angband going to lure away all
the NetHack players?

Thus, NetHack, in theory, could have a bugfix
release with all known bugs repaired, before a new
features release. That would provide to the world a
close-to-bulletproof release, and the
new-features-release could then be treated like the
beta release it undoubtedly will be.

Over the years, I've developed a really strong
revulsion for buggy software, to the point that my
test harness software toward the end of my
professional life was usually much bulkier than the
code that got delivered as "product".

All that said, I've only very rarely managed to
crash 3.4.3. It is quite robust for something as
complex as it is.

I just wish it were written in a maintainable
language, instead of in circa 1972 state of the art
compiler and programming language technology. There
are much, much better ways to write software today,
Java, Euclid, Haskal, and Ada among them. Even C++
isn't too horrid to use, if one is very, very
careful.

FWIW

